{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact f019da60ed08c10fce34123d32f1c2cf1296174f, tree 22da28008fd7adf512269e2049e334669b86fad5. All findings are below.\n\nF1 — P1 — Define the externally effective fact, send proof and receipt for prepare/commit. Section 5.2, lines 236–249, puts the original act/handler, effects and sends in the prepare entry, gives the commit entry no sends, and dispatches those sends “from the commit”. The existing delivery contract authenticates the exact source entry and requires that entry to hold the send at its ordinal (docs/scopes.md, Delivery; derive/src/delivery.ts sentBy). Thus a DeliveryCause naming the commit cannot prove its send. Naming the prepare instead supplies bytes containing a send that may later be abandoned and has not taken effect. The same distinction reaches create seeds/results and fact operands, not just dispatch scheduling. Existing fold indexes accepted act keys and decided deliveries on the original input entry (derive/src/fold.ts); settlement then returns that entry’s receipt (scope/src/core.ts settle). Merely deferring effects would therefore expose a prepare as an accepted act/decided delivery, including one later abandoned. This is a missing rule in the proposed design, not a claim that the unimplemented runtime already changed.\n\nChoose and state one effective-fact model. Specify which entry a send’s cause, creation seed, receipt, returned fact and idempotency/decided index name; how receivers and replay authenticate the prepare plus its successful commit/resolution; and how pending or abandoned prepares are barred from acting as applied facts. Add paired witnesses: a sequenced act sends/creates and its receiver/result/settlement verifies after commit, while before commit and after abandon the same prepared bytes cannot yield an applied fact or accepted settlement. Include lost commit replies and duplicate original delivery. Add the resulting contract/derive/delivery/replay work to the slices rather than retaining the unqualified “sends and effects are unchanged” claim.\n\nF2 — P1 — Reserve the new protocol’s settlement work before sealing a prepare. Section 5.4 step 6 reserves “its effects” but does not reserve the commit-or-abandon entry itself or define its capacity classification. With a sequenced act that has no effects/sends, that effect reservation can be zero. Near the journal limit a prepare can be durable and resolved while there is no admissible entry left for commit; the fence then remains even though the object and order scope are available. The existing closing-checkpoint reservation is not an implicit reservation for a new commit input. The repository’s reservation rule is that admitted work must retain capacity to record its end (derive/src/reserve.ts); a prepared input is new pending work that needs a finite native quote. Its retained pages/dependencies and delayed effects also need an admission/transfer rule across prepare, commit and abandon, so accounting and replay agree.\n\nState the reservation and its release/transfer in all applicable dimensions, and the equivalent settlement guarantee for each accepted order allocation’s resolution/hole/expiry. The allocation’s timed expiry may supply part of the order scope’s existing reservation, but the new platform request paths must explicitly consume the same duty rather than be classified as unrelated new work. Add low-capacity controls: refuse before prepare/allocation if settlement cannot fit; otherwise commit or abandon and order finalization remain recordable after eviction and while other members consume remaining free capacity. The failure model must distinguish object unavailability from a missing protocol reservation. This completes the capacity part of prior F1; the hash-bound judgment and local fence alone do not settle it.\n\nF3 — P2 — Align the S1 handoff with the amended late form. Sections 6.1 and 9 still prescribe late: \"refuse\" and describe the other alternative as a branch. The effective amendment b53643f2, reaffirmed by d66ec837, uses late:{within:{seconds},reason,then?:...}, with bound/reason common to both outcomes and absence of then selecting refusal. The old string form is the ambiguity the packages review requested removing. Replace the stale syntax in 6.1 and the S1 done condition, and cite the current amendment rather than only f92b3f2a. Keep strict boundary, clock-behind and precedence consistent with the companion note. This does not reopen the now-correct source-order suppression rule.\n\n1. Fidelity. Draft 3 substantially answers the five draft-2 findings: durable hash-bound preparation with a local fence; an explicit weaker availability contract for resolved-but-uncommitted positions; one genesis-fold/cut/leave rule including X=L; close requires no open allocations and final positions can settle from the journal; source order explicitly permits the lower-first run to apply both inputs. F1 and F2 above are consequences the newly introduced two-entry protocol still needs to define. There is no approval of S1–S5 runtime implementation.\n\n2. Correctness. The revised cut and closure rules are materially clearer, and the loss/eviction controls are appropriate. Approval remains withheld for the effective-fact and capacity gaps, plus the stale S1 syntax. I traced those gaps against the cited native source contracts; I did not infer that a successful prepare is already an applied commit, or assume the old runtime understands the new input kinds.\n\n3. Evidence and scope. I read the entire 840-line primary first, then the exact scopes paragraph and authored delta. Independent scratch verification materialized all 2,118 regular leaves and checked their Git blob identities: only the design note and docs/scopes.md differ from authored base feccbbcf; all other 2,116 leaves, including source/tests, are unchanged. Whitespace passes. Source/tests are unchanged between base tree b5a0a28f1b0809bf5b090689761ff5557f3c5998 and candidate tree 22da28008fd7adf512269e2049e334669b86fad5, so docs/testing.md’s documents-only exception applies and no unchanged gate or whole suite was repeated. Scratch: /private/tmp/checker-order-draft3-7zkvz71v/validation.json.\n\nThis branch is based on feccbbcf, not current Main. Any eventual landing must preserve the later Main changes. The note’s future memory/native/replay/clone witnesses are specifications, not executed evidence. The runtime, native capacity extension, package groups and archive binding remain future slices.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e605a4ef685787d14a16b49f6650333fab9bc6d4","binding":"assigned","head":"f019da60ed08c10fce34123d32f1c2cf1296174f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:418298f30627680f7619c73434ffb9eacae576a4\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3e4a926151f6bdb386dade53b343c35eef2496dd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80d6630a2f2867813a7af20b0856853372c469b3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}